ground_truth:
Using the quadratic formula, we find that the roots of the quadratic are $\frac{-5\pm\sqrt{5^2-4(3)(k)}}{6}=\frac{-5\pm\sqrt{25-12k}}{6}$. Since the problem tells us that these roots must equal $\frac{-5\pm i\sqrt{11}}{6}$, we have \begin{align*} \sqrt{25-12k}&=i\sqrt{11}
\\\Rightarrow\qquad \sqrt{25-12k}&=\sqrt{-11}
\\\Rightarrow\qquad 25-12k&=-11
\\\Rightarrow\qquad 12k&=36
\\\Rightarrow\qquad k&=\boxed{3}.
\end{align*}

First, we perform the operation in parentheses: \[6+5-4\times 3\div (2+1) = 6+5-4\times 3\div 3.\] Then, we perform multiplication and division, going from left to right: \begin{align*}
6+5-4\times 3\div 3 &= 6+5-12 \div 3 \\
&=6+5-4.
\end{align*} Finally, we perform addition and subtraction, going from left to right, to get our answer: \begin{align*}
6+5-4 &= 11-4 \\
&=\boxed{7}.
\end{align*}

